{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a896b814be936f89d","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Nvu4MHqXvHPxfiPJSTh4KC","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-u8ZfM0/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-RK3msz/db0c18e4-4025-4f1b-bc6c-7708c62dcd24/tasks/a896b814be936f89d.output","summary":"Read all eight target files plus the private headers, config, `strbuffer.c`, `memory.c`, `error.c`, `hashtable_seed.c`, `dtoa.c` (relevant parts), and the exported symbol table of `build/libjansson.so`. Everything below is derived from that source.\n\nBuild config in this tree (matters for several axes) — `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/c_src/include/jansson_config.h`: `JSON_INTEGER_IS_LONG_LONG 1`, `JSON_PARSER_MAX_DEPTH 2048`; `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/c_src/include/jansson_private_config.h`: `DTOA_ENABLED 1`, `INITIAL_HASHTABLE_ORDER 3`, `HAVE_UNISTD_H`, `HAVE_ATOMIC_BUILTINS`/`HAVE_SYNC_BUILTINS`, `USE_URANDOM 1`. Note `jansson_config.h` does **not** define `JSON_HAVE_ATOMIC_BUILTINS`/`JSON_HAVE_SYNC_BUILTINS`, so the public `json_incref`/`json_decref` inlines use plain `++/--` refcounting (`jansson.h:117-120`), while `hashtable_seed.c` does use the atomics.\n\n---\n\n# 1. DECODE FLAGS (`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/c_src/src/load.c`)\n\nValues: `JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES 0x1`, `JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK 0x2`, `JSON_DECODE_ANY 0x4`, `JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL 0x8`, `JSON_ALLOW_NUL 0x10` (`jansson.h:358-362`). All five reach the lexer/parser via the `flags` parameter; only `JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL` is read out of `lex->flags`. Unknown/extra bits are ignored silently, so decode and encode flag words may be mixed with no effect.\n\n**JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES** — `load.c:691` (inside `parse_object`, after key is stolen and NUL-checked):\n```c\n        if (flags & JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES) {\n            if (json_object_getn(object, key, len)) {\n                jsonp_free(key);\n                error_set(error, lex, json_error_duplicate_key, \"duplicate object key\");\n                goto error;\n            }\n        }\n```\nOff: `json_object_setn_new_nocheck` → `hashtable_set` overwrites (`hashtable.c:243-245`), last value wins, insertion position of the *first* occurrence is retained (the `ordered_list` node is not re-inserted). On: first repeat of a key aborts the parse with `json_error_duplicate_key`. Comparison is length-aware `memcmp` on the decoded (post-escape) key, so `\"a\"` vs `\"\\u0061\"` collide.\n\n**JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK** — `load.c:867` in `parse_json`:\n```c\n    if (!(flags & JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK)) {\n        lex_scan(lex, error);\n        if (lex->token != TOKEN_EOF) {\n            error_set(error, lex, json_error_end_of_input_expected,\n                      \"end of file expected\");\n```\nOff (default): one extra `lex_scan` must yield `TOKEN_EOF`; trailing garbage (or a second value) → `json_error_end_of_input_expected` and the already-built value is `json_decref`'d. On: parsing stops right after the first complete value; trailing bytes ignored. Note the extra `lex_scan` also consumes/looks ahead one token — with the check disabled, `error->position` (set at `load.c:877-880`) is the position *after the value only*, which is what makes streaming multiple values from one FILE*/fd possible.\n\n**JSON_DECODE_ANY** — `load.c:856`:\n```c\n    if (!(flags & JSON_DECODE_ANY)) {\n        if (lex->token != '[' && lex->token != '{') {\n            error_set(error, lex, json_error_invalid_syntax, \"'[' or '{' expected\");\n```\nOff: top level must be array or object. On: any of string / integer / real / `true` / `false` / `null` is accepted as the root (and the empty input `\"\"` still gives `json_error_premature_end_of_input` via the `error_set` remapping at `load.c:118-121`).\n\n**JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL** — `load.c:496` in `lex_scan_number`:\n```c\n    if (!(lex->flags & JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL) && c != '.' && c != 'E' && c != 'e') {\n        json_int_t intval;\n        ...\n        intval = json_strtoint(saved_text, &end, 10);\n        if (errno == ERANGE) { ... \"too big negative integer\" / \"too big integer\" ... }\n        lex->token = TOKEN_INTEGER;\n```\nOff: pure-integer literals go through `strtoll` and become `TOKEN_INTEGER`; `ERANGE` → `json_error_numeric_overflow`. On: the integer fast path is skipped entirely, so the `.`/`e` sections are skipped (no fractional/exponent chars present) and the text goes to `jsonp_strtod` → `TOKEN_REAL` → `json_real()`. Consequences: `12345678901234567890` no longer errors (it becomes a rounded double), overflow now reports `\"real number overflow\"` from `load.c:551-553` instead, and every integer in the document becomes `JSON_REAL` (changes `json_is_integer`, `unpack 'i'/'I'` validation, and re-encoding to `X.0` form).\n\n**JSON_ALLOW_NUL** — `load.c:790` in `parse_value`, `case TOKEN_STRING`:\n```c\n            if (!(flags & JSON_ALLOW_NUL)) {\n                if (memchr(value, '\\0', len)) {\n                    error_set(error, lex, json_error_null_character,\n                              \"\\\\u0000 is not allowed without JSON_ALLOW_NUL\");\n                    return NULL;\n                }\n            }\n```\nOnly affects string **values**. Object **keys** are unconditionally rejected earlier at `load.c:684-689` (`json_error_null_byte_in_key`), regardless of this flag. A *raw* 0x00 byte inside a string is never allowed by either flag — it is caught as a control char in `lex_scan_string` (`load.c:317`, message `\"control character 0x0\"`). Only the `\\u0000` escape is unlocked. With the flag on, the resulting `json_t` holds an embedded NUL and length-aware APIs (`json_string_length`, `json_stringn`, `json_object_getn`) are required to handle it.\n\n---\n\n# 2. ENCODE FLAGS (`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/c_src/src/dump.c`)\n\nBit layout (`jansson.h:381-390`): indent = bits 0-4 (`JSON_MAX_INDENT 0x1F`), `JSON_COMPACT 0x20`, `JSON_ENSURE_ASCII 0x40`, `JSON_SORT_KEYS 0x80`, `JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER 0x100`, `JSON_ENCODE_ANY 0x200`, `JSON_ESCAPE_SLASH 0x400`, real precision = bits 11-15 (`((n) & 0x1F) << 11`), `JSON_EMBED 0x10000` (bit 16). Decoders read the same low bits, so the words are not interchangeable.\n\n**JSON_INDENT(n) / JSON_MAX_INDENT** — `dump.c:29` and `dump.c:71-92`:\n```c\n#define FLAGS_TO_INDENT(f)    ((f) & 0x1F)\n...\nstatic int dump_indent(size_t flags, int depth, int space, json_dump_callback_t dump, void *data) {\n    if (FLAGS_TO_INDENT(flags) > 0) {\n        unsigned int ws_count = FLAGS_TO_INDENT(flags), n_spaces = depth * ws_count;\n        if (dump(\"\\n\", 1, data)) return -1;\n        while (n_spaces > 0) {\n            int cur_n = n_spaces < sizeof whitespace - 1 ? n_spaces : sizeof whitespace - 1;\n            if (dump(whitespace, cur_n, data)) return -1;\n            n_spaces -= cur_n;\n        }\n    } else if (space && !(flags & JSON_COMPACT)) {\n        return dump(\" \", 1, data);\n    }\n```\n`n` is masked to 0-31; `JSON_INDENT(0)` and `JSON_INDENT(32)` are both \"no indent\". `whitespace[]` is 32 spaces (`dump.c:69`), and total indentation `depth * ws_count` is emitted in ≤32-byte chunks — so deep nesting × indent 31 produces many callback invocations per line (a distinct shape from small indents). `dump_indent` is called at container open (`space=0`), after every comma (`space=1`), and before the closing bracket at the parent depth (`space=0`).\n\n**JSON_COMPACT** — two sites. `dump.c:88` (above): with indent==0 it suppresses the single space after commas. `dump.c:311-317`:\n```c\n            if (flags & JSON_COMPACT) {\n                separator = \":\";\n                separator_length = 1;\n            } else {\n                separator = \": \";\n                separator_length = 2;\n            }\n```\nThe `key: value` separator is governed **only** by `JSON_COMPACT`, independent of indent. Interaction: when indent > 0, the `dump_indent` compact branch is dead (the `if` wins), so `JSON_INDENT(4)|JSON_COMPACT` still pretty-prints newlines/indents but uses `\":\"` instead of `\": \"`. Compact alone (indent 0) yields `{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}`; indent 0 without compact yields `{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}`.\n\n**JSON_ENSURE_ASCII** — `dump.c:123` inside `dump_string`'s scan loop:\n```c\n            /* non-ASCII */\n            if ((flags & JSON_ENSURE_ASCII) && codepoint > 0x7F)\n                break;\n```\nBreaking out routes the codepoint to the `default:` case at `dump.c:164-186`: BMP codepoints → `\"\\\\u%04X\"` (length 6), non-BMP → recomputed UTF-16 surrogate pair `\"\\\\u%04X\\\\u%04X\"` (length 12, `first = 0xD800 | ((cp & 0xffc00) >> 10)`, `last = 0xDC00 | (cp & 0x3ff)`). Off: multi-byte UTF-8 is copied through verbatim in runs. Either way `utf8_iterate` must succeed — an invalid-UTF-8 string built via a `*_nocheck` API makes `dump_string` return -1 (`dump.c:110-112`).\n\n**JSON_SORT_KEYS** — `dump.c:335`:\n```c\n            if (flags & JSON_SORT_KEYS) {\n                struct key_len *keys;\n                ...\n                keys = jsonp_malloc(size * sizeof(struct key_len));\n                if (!keys) return -1;\n                ... collect all (key,len) ...\n                assert(i == size);\n                qsort(keys, size, sizeof(struct key_len), compare_keys);\n```\nSort order is `compare_keys` (`dump.c:203-213`): `memcmp` over `min(len1,len2)` then `k1->len - k2->len` — raw byte order, prefix-shorter-first, NOT locale/codepoint-aware beyond UTF-8's natural byte order. `struct key_len.len` is `int` (`dump.c:200`). Each sorted key is re-looked-up with `json_object_getn` plus `assert(value)`. Also note `dump_string(key,...)`'s return value is discarded in both the sorted (`dump.c:366`) and unsorted (`dump.c:396`) key paths.\n\n**JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER** — defined at `jansson.h:386` but **referenced nowhere** in any `.c` file (verified by exhaustive grep). It is a no-op: insertion order is always preserved by `hashtable`'s `ordered_list` (`hashtable.c:253`, `hashtable_iter*`).\n\n**JSON_ENCODE_ANY** — `dump.c:485` in `json_dump_callback` (the only place, so it applies to all six dump entry points):\n```c\n    if (!(flags & JSON_ENCODE_ANY)) {\n        if (!json_is_array(json) && !json_is_object(json))\n            return -1;\n    }\n```\nOff: scalar roots fail with -1 before any output. On: scalars are dumped (`\"str\"`, `42`, `1.0`, `true`, `false`, `null`). `json == NULL` always fails (`dump.c:221`).\n\n**JSON_ESCAPE_SLASH** — `dump.c:119`:\n```c\n            /* slash */\n            if ((flags & JSON_ESCAPE_SLASH) && codepoint == '/')\n                break;\n```\nplus the `case '/': text = \"\\\\/\";` arm at `dump.c:161-163`. Off, `/` is emitted literally; on, as `\\/`.\n\n**JSON_REAL_PRECISION(n)** — `dump.c:30` and `dump.c:246-257`:\n```c\n#define FLAGS_TO_PRECISION(f) (((f) >> 11) & 0x1F)\n...\n            size = jsonp_dtostr(buffer, MAX_REAL_STR_LENGTH, value, FLAGS_TO_PRECISION(flags));\n            if (size < 0) return -1;\n```\n`MAX_REAL_STR_LENGTH` is 25 (`dump.c:27`). Precision is masked to 0-31 (`JSON_REAL_PRECISION(32) == 0`). In the `DTOA_ENABLED` path (`strconv.c:69-168`): `int mode = precision == 0 ? 0 : 2;` — precision 0 = dtoa mode 0 = shortest round-trip representation; precision ≥1 = mode 2 = that many significant digits. Two hard failure modes, both surfacing as an encode error (`do_dump` → -1):\n- `dtoa_r(..., digits, 25)` with a 25-byte scratch: mode 2 sets `i = ndigits` and `dtoa_r` bails at `dtoa.c:5341` (`else if (blen <= (size_t)i) { ... return 0; }`), so **precision 25-31 always returns -1** → dumping any real fails.\n- the explicit size guard at `strconv.c:101-112` (`3 + (vdigits_end - vdigits_start) + (use_exp ? 5 : 0) > size`) rejects outputs that don't fit in 25 bytes, e.g. small-magnitude values (`decpt <= 0`) with large precision.\n\nExponent-vs-fixed selection is at `strconv.c:86-90`: `if (decpt <= -4 || decpt > 16) { use_exp = 1; exp = decpt - 1; decpt = 1; }`. Integral values get a forced `\".0\"` (`strconv.c:95-96`, and the trailing `if (p[-1] == '.') p--;` at 157). (The non-DTOA fallback at `strconv.c:185-236` maps precision 0 → 17, uses `\"%.*g\"`, appends `\".0\"` if neither `.` nor `e` is present, and strips `+`/leading zeros from the exponent — different but observably similar output.)\n\n**JSON_EMBED** — `dump.c:217-219`, and consumed at `dump.c:277-281`, `dump.c:295-301`, `dump.c:324-330`, `dump.c:416`:\n```c\n    int embed = flags & JSON_EMBED;\n    flags &= ~JSON_EMBED;\n```\nBecause the bit is stripped before recursion, `JSON_EMBED` applies **only to the root value**. Effects by root type:\n- array: `if (!embed && dump(\"[\", 1, data))` suppresses `[`, and each of the three return points becomes `return embed ? 0 : dump(\"]\", 1, data)` — so an embedded array emits only `elem, elem, ...`. An **empty** embedded array emits nothing at all and returns 0 (`dump.c:279-282`).\n- object: same for `{` / `}` (`dump.c:324`, `dump.c:330`, `dump.c:416`); empty embedded object emits nothing.\n- scalars: `embed` is never consulted in the NULL/TRUE/FALSE/INTEGER/REAL/STRING arms, so `JSON_EMBED` is silently ignored for scalar roots (still subject to the `JSON_ENCODE_ANY` gate).\nIndentation is still computed from `depth` starting at 0 (`json_dump_callback` passes `depth=0`), so an embedded container's items are indented one level in.\n\nOther encode-side shape notes: `JSON_INTEGER` uses a 25-byte buffer and fails if `size < 0 || size >= MAX_INTEGER_STR_LENGTH` (`dump.c:234-244`) — `long long` needs at most 20 bytes, so this cannot trip with `JSON_INTEGER_IS_LONG_LONG`. Cycles are caught by `jsonp_loop_check` (`dump.c:272`, `dump.c:320`) keyed on `snprintf(key,\"%p\",json)` into a `char key[2 + sizeof(ptr)*2 + 1]` / `LOOP_KEY_LEN` buffer → self-referential containers make dumping return -1.\n\n---\n\n# 3. PACK / UNPACK (`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/c_src/src/pack_unpack.c`)\n\n`JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY 0x1`, `JSON_STRICT 0x2` (`jansson.h:332-333`). Both are unpack-only; `json_vpack_ex` stores `flags` in the scanner but `pack*()` never reads it.\n\n**JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY** — 8 sites, one per value-consuming unpack case (`pack_unpack.c:686, 723, 738, 753, 768, 783, 792, 797`), all of the form:\n```c\n            if (!(s->flags & JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY)) {\n                int *target = va_arg(*ap, int *);\n                if (root) *target = (int)json_integer_value(root);\n            }\n```\nand for `'O'`:\n```c\n        case 'O':\n            if (root && !(s->flags & JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY))\n                json_incref(root);\n            /* Fall through */\n        case 'o':\n            if (!(s->flags & JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY)) {\n                json_t **target = va_arg(*ap, json_t **);\n```\nWith the flag set, **no varargs are consumed for values** (so you pass the format only) and type checks still run; `'O'` also does not incref. Exceptions that consume varargs regardless of the flag: object **keys** (`key = va_arg(*ap, const char *)` at `pack_unpack.c:520`) and the `'s%'` length pointer is inside the guard, so it is not consumed either. `'n'` never consumes an argument in any mode (`pack_unpack.c:805-812`, \"Never assign, just validate\").\n\n**JSON_STRICT** — exactly two sites, one per container:\n```c\n    if (strict == 0 && (s->flags & JSON_STRICT))   /* pack_unpack.c:553, unpack_object */\n        strict = 1;\n    if (strict == 0 && (s->flags & JSON_STRICT))   /* pack_unpack.c:658, unpack_array  */\n        strict = 1;\n```\ni.e. `JSON_STRICT` makes `!` the default for *every* container in the format, recursively. An explicit `*` inside a container sets `strict = -1` (`pack_unpack.c:509`, `pack_unpack.c:628`) and therefore locally overrides `JSON_STRICT`. Enforcement: object — `pack_unpack.c:556-591`, iterates `json_object_keylen_foreach` when `gotopt || json_object_size(root) != key_set.size`, and reports `\"%li object item(s) left unpacked: %s\"` with a comma-joined list of unrecognized keys built in a `strbuffer_t` (falls back to `\"<unknown>\"` on OOM); array — `pack_unpack.c:661-666`, `i != json_array_size(root)` → `\"%li array item(s) left unpacked\"`. Both use code `json_error_end_of_input_expected`. Also note `!`/`*` must be the *last* token before the closing bracket; anything after → `\"Expected '}' after '%c', got '%c'\"` (`pack_unpack.c:495-500`, `614-619`).\n\n**PACK format characters** (`pack()`, `pack_unpack.c:423-461`) — vararg types consumed:\n\n| char | vararg consumed | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{` … `}` | — | `pack_object`; keys must be `s` (`pack_unpack.c:230`) |\n| `[` … `]` | — | `pack_array` |\n| `s` | `const char *` | `json_stringn_nocheck` on the arg; UTF-8 validated by `utf8_check_string` (`pack_unpack.c:145`) |\n| `n` | — | always `json_null()` |\n| `b` | `int` | nonzero → `json_true()`, else `json_false()` |\n| `i` | `int` | `pack_integer` |\n| `I` | `json_int_t` (`long long` here) | `pack_integer` |\n| `f` | `double` | `pack_real`; `json_real_set` rejects NaN/Inf → `json_error_numeric_overflow` \"Invalid floating point value\" (`pack_unpack.c:410-418`) |\n| `O` | `json_t *` | increfs (`pack_object_inter(..., 1)`) |\n| `o` | `json_t *` | steals the reference (`pack_object_inter(..., 0)`) |\n\nPack modifiers (`read_string`, `pack_unpack.c:119-211`; `pack_string`, `330-357`; `pack_object_inter`, `359-386`):\n- `s#` → extra `int` length: `length = va_arg(*ap, int);` (`pack_unpack.c:176`) — note it is read as `int` and stored in a `size_t`.\n- `s%` → extra `size_t` length: `length = va_arg(*ap, size_t);` (`pack_unpack.c:178`).\n- `s+` / `s+#` / `s+%` → concatenation loop (`pack_unpack.c:166-194`): each `+` pulls another `const char *`, optionally followed by its own `#` (`int`) or `%` (`size_t`); pieces are appended to a `strbuffer_t` and UTF-8-checked **once on the concatenated result** (`pack_unpack.c:201`), then ownership transfers via `strbuffer_steal_value` + `jsonp_stringn_nocheck_own`.\n- `s?` → optional string: NULL arg yields `json_null()` (`pack_unpack.c:346`). `s*` → optional: NULL arg yields NULL, which the enclosing `pack_object`/`pack_array` treats as \"skip this item\" (`pack_unpack.c:249-255`, `302-307`).\n- Combining optional with length/concat is rejected: `\"Cannot use '%c' on optional strings\"` (`pack_unpack.c:153-159`).\n- `O?` / `o?` → NULL arg yields `json_null()`; `O*` / `o*` → NULL arg yields NULL → item skipped (`pack_unpack.c:374-381`). Without `?`/`*`, NULL → `json_error_null_value` \"NULL object\".\n- In `pack_object`, a NULL *value* when the value spec was not `*` sets `json_error_null_value` \"NULL object value\" (`pack_unpack.c:249-252`).\n- Separators `' '`, `'\\t'`, `'\\n'`, `','`, `':'` are skipped by `next_token` (`pack_unpack.c:76-85`) and are purely cosmetic; `'\\n'` bumps the reported line number. Anything else → `json_error_invalid_format` \"Unexpected format character '%c'\".\n- Format-level errors: NULL/empty fmt → `json_error_invalid_argument` (`pack_unpack.c:826-831`); leftover tokens after the root value → \"Garbage after format string\" (`pack_unpack.c:845-851`).\n\n**UNPACK format characters** (`unpack()`, `pack_unpack.c:671-819`):\n\n| char | vararg consumed (unless `JSON_VALIDATE_ONLY`) | type check |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{` … `}` | keys: `const char *` each (always) | `json_is_object` |\n| `[` … `]` | — | `json_is_array` |\n| `s` | `const char **` | `json_is_string`; NULL target → `json_error_null_value` |\n| `s%` | `const char **`, then `size_t *` | length via `json_string_length`; NULL length target errors |\n| `i` | `int *` | `json_is_integer`; value cast `(int)json_integer_value` |\n| `I` | `json_int_t *` | `json_is_integer` |\n| `b` | `int *` | `json_is_boolean`; stores `json_is_true(root)` |\n| `f` | `double *` | `json_is_real` — **rejects integers** |\n| `F` | `double *` | `json_is_number` (real *or* integer), stores `json_number_value` |\n| `O` | `json_t **` | no type check; increfs |\n| `o` | `json_t **` | no type check; borrows |\n| `n` | none, ever | `json_is_null` |\n\nUnpack modifiers:\n- `s%` is the only length modifier on the unpack side; `'#'` is **not** accepted — `unpack` only peeks for `'%'` (`pack_unpack.c:698`) and an `'#'` then falls through to the container loop where `strchr(unpack_value_starters, '#')` fails → \"Unexpected format character '#'\". Likewise `+` is pack-only.\n- `?` is an **object-only** modifier and sits between the key and the value spec: `{s?i}` (`pack_unpack.c:529-532`, `opt = gotopt = 1`). A missing key with `?` leaves the target untouched (`value = NULL`, and each case guards assignment with `if (root)`); a missing key without `?` → `json_error_item_not_found` \"Object item not found: %s\".\n- `!` (strict, `strict = 1`) and `*` (non-strict, `strict = -1`) are valid in both `{...!}` and `[...!]` (`pack_unpack.c:508-512`, `627-631`).\n- Array elements must be one of `unpack_value_starters` = `\"{[siIbfFOon\"` (`pack_unpack.c:42`, checked at `pack_unpack.c:633`); note `'F'` is in that set and `'!'`/`'*'` are handled earlier. Object values are *not* pre-validated against that set — an unknown char there falls to `unpack`'s `default:` error.\n- Nested skipping: once `root` is NULL (only reachable via optional-missing keys), the sub-format is walked with `value = NULL` and nothing is assigned but format validity and vararg consumption still occur.\n- Array index beyond the array → `json_error_index_out_of_range` \"Array index %lu out of range\" (`pack_unpack.c:644-648`).\n- `json_vunpack_ex` rejects NULL root (`json_error_null_value`) and NULL/empty fmt (`json_error_invalid_argument`), and errors on trailing format tokens (\"Garbage after format string\", `pack_unpack.c:907-912`).\n- The strict-checking key set is a `hashtable_t` populated with `hashtable_set(&key_set, key, key_len, json_null())` (`pack_unpack.c:549`) — so the same key unpacked twice counts once, which is exactly why `json_object_size(root) != key_set.size` is not sufficient and `gotopt` forces the full scan.\n\n---\n\n# 4. INPUT SHAPES THE CODE SPECIAL-CASES\n\n**Empty object / empty array (decode)**: `parse_object` returns immediately on `'}'` as the first token (`load.c:668-669`); `parse_array` on `']'` (`load.c:745-746`). Note `parse_array`'s loop condition is `while (lex->token)` — token 0 is `TOKEN_EOF`, so unterminated input exits the loop and hits the `\"']' expected\"` error.\n\n**Empty object / array (encode)**: array uses `n == 0` (`dump.c:279`), object uses `iter == NULL` from `json_object_iter` (`dump.c:328`) — not `json_object_size`. Neither emits indentation inside, so output is exactly `[]` / `{}` (or nothing under `JSON_EMBED`).\n\n**Single element vs many (encode)**: `dump.c:286-295` loops `for (i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i)` and then dumps element `i` (== `n-1`) after the loop, so `n == 1` skips the loop entirely and never emits a comma; the closing `dump_indent(flags, depth, 0, ...)` is emitted at parent depth. Objects use the `next != NULL` lookahead (`dump.c:392`, `402`) or the `i < size - 1` test in the sorted path (`dump.c:373`) for the same purpose.\n\n**Hashtable growth (objects) — exact numbers**: `INITIAL_HASHTABLE_ORDER 3` (`hashtable.c:23-25`, also set in `jansson_private_config.h`) → `hashsize(3) == 8` buckets at init (`hashtable.c:178-188`). Growth test at the *top* of `hashtable_set` (`hashtable.c:233-236`):\n```c\n    /* rehash if the load ratio exceeds 1 */\n    if (hashtable->size >= hashsize(hashtable->order))\n        if (hashtable_do_rehash(hashtable))\n            return -1;\n```\n`hashtable_do_rehash` does `new_order = order + 1` (doubling). So rehashes occur when `size` reaches **8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, …** — i.e. while attempting to insert the 9th, 17th, 33rd, 65th, … distinct key (order 3→4→5→6→7…). The check fires even if the key already exists (an overwrite of the 8th key still triggers a rehash). Bucket indexing uses `hash & hashmask(order)` in set/get/del but `pair->hash % new_size` in the rehash (`hashtable.c:167`) — equivalent only because sizes are powers of two. Very long keys: `init_pair` refuses `key_len >= (size_t)-1 - offsetof(pair_t, key)` (`hashtable.c:205-208`).\n\n**Array growth**: `json_array()` starts with `size = 8`, `entries = 0` (`value.c:437-439`); `json_array_grow` uses `new_size = max(array->size + amount, array->size * 2)` (`value.c:519`) → capacities 8, 16, 32, 64, … for appends, but a single `json_array_extend` of a larger array jumps straight to `size + amount`. `json_array_insert_new` at `index == entries` skips the `memmove` (`value.c:575`); `json_array_remove` of the last index skips it too (`value.c:596-598`).\n\n**strbuffer growth** (affects `json_dumps`, lexer `saved_text`, pack `s+`): `STRBUFFER_MIN_SIZE 16`, `STRBUFFER_FACTOR 2`, `new_size = max(size*2, length+size+1)` with explicit overflow guards (`strbuffer.c:17-19`, `61-79`).\n\n**String with embedded NUL**: decode requires `JSON_ALLOW_NUL` and only via `\\u0000`; keys never allow it. Encode: `codepoint < 0x20` → `default:` arm → `\"\\\\u0000\"`. Internally strings are length-carrying (`json_string_t.length`, `jansson_private.h:47-51`) and `json_string_equal` uses `memcmp` over `length` (`value.c:826`). `json_loads` itself stops at the first raw NUL in the C string (`string_get`, `load.c:890-900`), while `json_loadb` honors `buflen` and will therefore *see* raw NULs (→ control-char syntax error).\n\n**Non-ASCII / multi-byte UTF-8 (2/3/4-byte)**: `utf8_check_first` (`utf.c:38-67`) classifies: `<0x80` → 1; `0x80-0xBF` → 0 (stray continuation); `0xC0`/`0xC1` → 0 (overlong); `0xC2-0xDF` → 2; `0xE0-0xEF` → 3; `0xF0-0xF4` → 4; `>=0xF5` → 0. `utf8_check_full` (`utf.c:69-114`) rejects `value > 0x10FFFF`, `0xD800..0xDFFF`, and overlongs (`size==2 && value<0x80`, `size==3 && value<0x800`, `size==4 && value<0x10000`). The decoder reads whole sequences into `stream_t.buffer[5]` (`load.c:157-186`) — if the stream ends mid-sequence the missing bytes come back as `EOF` and `utf8_check_full` fails → `STREAM_STATE_ERROR` + `json_error_invalid_utf8` \"unable to decode byte 0x%x\". Column counting only increments on lead bytes (`load.c:195-199`), so column is a *character* count while `position` is a byte count.\n\n**Surrogate escapes \\uD800-\\uDFFF (decode, `load.c:383-414`)**: a high surrogate `0xD800-0xDBFF` must be followed immediately by `\\u` + a low surrogate `0xDC00-0xDFFF`, combined as `((value - 0xD800) << 10) + (value2 - 0xDC00) + 0x10000`. Otherwise: `\"invalid Unicode '\\\\u%04X\\\\u%04X'\"` (bad second half) or `\"invalid Unicode '\\\\u%04X'\"` (no second half). A lone low surrogate `0xDC00-0xDFFF` is rejected outright. Escapes are validated twice: hex-digit shape in the scanning pass (`load.c:330-339`), semantics in the decoding pass (`decode_unicode_escape`, `load.c:274-294`, which returns -1 for a non-hex digit). Encode side reconstructs surrogate pairs only under `JSON_ENSURE_ASCII` for cp ≥ 0x10000 (`dump.c:171-182`).\n\n**Control characters**: decode rejects raw `0x00-0x1F` inside strings (`load.c:317-326`) with a special message for `'\\n'` (\"unexpected newline\") vs everything else (\"control character 0x%x\"), and `lex_unget_unsave` rewinds first so the error position points at the offending byte. Valid escapes are exactly `\" \\ / b f n r t u` (`load.c:340-346`). Encode escapes `\\\\`, `\"`, and everything `< 0x20`, with short forms for `\\b \\f \\n \\r \\t` and `\\u00XX` for the rest (`dump.c:115`, `139-169`) — `0x7F` (DEL) is *not* escaped unless `JSON_ENSURE_ASCII` is set (it is `> 0x7F`? no — `0x7F` is not `> 0x7F`, so DEL is always emitted raw).\n\n**Integer boundaries**: `JSON_INTEGER_IS_LONG_LONG 1` → `json_int_t` = `long long`, `JSON_INTEGER_FORMAT \"lld\"`, `json_strtoint` = `strtoll` (`jansson.h:67-77`, `load.c:459-469`). Boundary values: `9223372036854775807` / `-9223372036854775808` decode; one past either → `errno == ERANGE` → `json_error_numeric_overflow`, with the sign of the clamped result selecting `\"too big negative integer\"` vs `\"too big integer\"` (`load.c:505-512`). `assert(end == saved_text + lex->saved_text.length)` at `load.c:514` relies on the number grammar having already validated the text. Grammar details worth exercising: leading zeros rejected (`0` followed by a digit, `load.c:481-486`), a bare `-` rejected, `+1`/`.5`/`1.`/`1e`/`1e+` rejected (`load.c:521-547`), `-0` is integer `0`. Encoding uses a 25-byte buffer with a `size >= MAX_INTEGER_STR_LENGTH` bail-out (`dump.c:238-241`).\n\n**Reals / doubles**: `json_real`/`json_real_set` reject NaN and ±Inf (`value.c:922-923`, `942-943`) — the only value-level rejection in the whole value API. `jsonp_strtod` (`strconv.c:45-62`) returns -1 only when `(value == HUGE_VAL || value == -HUGE_VAL) && errno == ERANGE` → `\"real number overflow\"`; **underflow to 0 is silently accepted** (`1e-400` → `0.0`, no error). Locale handling: `get_decimal_point()` sniffs the locale via `sprintf(\"%#.0f\", 1.0)` and `to_locale`/`from_locale` swap the single decimal separator character (`strconv.c:24-43`, `170-183`). Encode-side shapes: `0.0` → `\"0.0\"`, `-0.0` keeps its sign (`sign == 1` branch, `strconv.c:115-117`), exponent form chosen when `decpt <= -4 || decpt > 16` (`strconv.c:86`) with exponent printed via `sprintf(p, \"%d\", exp)` (no `+`, no zero-padding), and integral doubles are forced to have `.0`. Precision axis: 0 → dtoa mode 0 (shortest round-trip, 18-digit scratch), 1-24 → mode 2 with that many significant digits, **25-31 → always fails** (`dtoa.c:5341` with `blen == 25`), plus the `strconv.c:101-112` fit check that can fail for small-magnitude values at high precision.\n\n**Deep nesting**: `parse_value` (`load.c:776-783`):\n```c\n    lex->depth++;\n    if (lex->depth > JSON_PARSER_MAX_DEPTH) {\n        error_set(error, lex, json_error_stack_overflow, \"maximum parsing depth reached\");\n        return NULL;\n    }\n```\nLimit is 2048 here; `lex->depth` is reset to 0 per `parse_json` call (`load.c:853`) and decremented only on the success path (`load.c:846`). So depth 2048 parses, 2049 gives `json_error_stack_overflow`. Encoding has **no** depth limit — `do_dump` recurses freely (only the cycle check bounds it), so a 2048-deep tree recurses 2048 frames on dump.\n\n**Duplicate keys / key ordering**: see `JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES` above. Ordering is insertion order via `hashtable`'s separate `ordered_list` (`hashtable.c:253`, `hashtable_iter*` at `hashtable.c:294-333`); `hashtable_set` on an existing key replaces the value in place and does **not** move it in the ordered list. `hashtable_clear` resets both lists and `size = 0` but keeps the current (grown) bucket array (`hashtable.c:280-292`), so a cleared-and-refilled object does not shrink back to 8 buckets. `hashtable_del` returns -1 for a missing key. Hash is `hashlittle(key, len, hashtable_seed)` (`hashtable.c:38`, `lookup3.h`) — bucket iteration order (and therefore `JSON_SORT_KEYS`-off dump order across *different* insertion orders) is seed-dependent only for the internal chain, never for the ordered iteration.\n\n**Hash seed axis**: `json_object()` auto-seeds on first use if `hashtable_seed == 0` (`value.c:69-72`). `json_object_seed(0)` → `generate_seed()` (urandom → timestamp+pid fallback, `hashtable_seed.c:167-192`); `json_object_seed(n != 0)` → deterministic seed `(uint32_t)n`, but only if the seed has not already been set (`if (hashtable_seed == 0)` at `hashtable_seed.c:202`, etc.) — so seeding is effectively one-shot and must precede the first `json_object()`.\n\n**Other value-level branch axes**: `json_object_setn_new_nocheck` rejects `json == value` (self-insert) and non-objects (`value.c:131-134`); the same self-check exists in `json_array_set_new` / `append_new` / `insert_new`. `json_object_setn_new` (checked variant) validates the key with `utf8_check_string`. `json_equal` short-circuits on pointer identity (covers the `true`/`false`/`null` singletons, which have `refcount == (size_t)-1` and are never freed, `value.c:973-986`). `json_copy` returns the *same* pointer for true/false/null. `json_deep_copy`/`json_object_update_recursive` share the `jsonp_loop_check` cycle guard, so cycles yield NULL/-1. `jsonp_malloc(0)` returns NULL (`memory.c:25-30`), which is why a zero-size allocation path (e.g. `JSON_SORT_KEYS` on a size-0 object) matters — though the empty-object early return prevents it in `dump.c`.\n\n---\n\n# 5. PUBLIC ENTRY POINTS (grouped; L = low-level, W = convenience wrapper)\n\nVerified against `nm -D --defined-only build/libjansson.so`.\n\n**Construction / lifetime** — `json_object` (L), `json_array` (L), `json_string` (W over `json_stringn`), `json_stringn` (L, UTF-8 checked), `json_string_nocheck` (W over `json_stringn_nocheck`), `json_stringn_nocheck` (L), `json_integer` (L), `json_real` (L, rejects NaN/Inf), `json_true`/`json_false`/`json_null` (L, immortal singletons), `json_delete` (L, \"do not call directly\"). Header-only inlines (not symbols): `json_incref`, `json_decref`, `json_decrefp`, `json_boolean`, `json_typeof`/`json_is_*`, `json_boolean_value`.\n\n**Object API** — low-level/length-aware: `json_object_getn`, `json_object_setn_new`, `json_object_setn_new_nocheck`, `json_object_deln`, `json_object_size`, `json_object_clear`. Wrappers over them: `json_object_get`, `json_object_set_new`, `json_object_set_new_nocheck`, `json_object_del` (each just `strlen`s the key). Inline wrappers in the header: `json_object_set`, `json_object_setn`, `json_object_set_nocheck`, `json_object_setn_nocheck`, `json_object_iter_set`, `json_object_update_new`, `json_object_update_existing_new`, `json_object_update_missing_new`. Bulk: `json_object_update`, `json_object_update_existing`, `json_object_update_missing`, `json_object_update_recursive` (W over the exported-but-internal `do_object_update_recursive`). Iteration: `json_object_iter`, `json_object_iter_at`, `json_object_iter_next`, `json_object_iter_key`, `json_object_iter_key_len`, `json_object_iter_value`, `json_object_iter_set_new`, `json_object_key_to_iter`, plus the `json_object_foreach` / `json_object_keylen_foreach` / `*_safe` macros. Seeding: `json_object_seed`.\n\n**Array API** — `json_array_size`, `json_array_get`, `json_array_set_new`, `json_array_append_new`, `json_array_insert_new`, `json_array_remove`, `json_array_clear`, `json_array_extend` (all L). Inline wrappers: `json_array_set`, `json_array_append`, `json_array_insert`; macro `json_array_foreach`.\n\n**Scalar accessors** — `json_string_value`, `json_string_length`, `json_integer_value`, `json_real_value`, `json_number_value` (L), `json_string_setn`/`json_string_setn_nocheck` (L) with `json_string_set`/`json_string_set_nocheck` as `strlen` wrappers, `json_integer_set`, `json_real_set`.\n\n**Decoding** — `json_loadb` (L, buffer+len), `json_loads` (L, NUL-terminated), `json_loadf` (L, FILE* via `fgetc`), `json_loadfd` (L, fd via 1-byte `read`), `json_load_callback` (L), `json_load_file` (W: `fopen` + `json_loadf` + `fclose`).\n\n**Encoding** — `json_dump_callback` (L, the single real implementation), `json_dumps` (W, strbuffer), `json_dumpb` (W, fixed buffer), `json_dumpf` (W, FILE*), `json_dumpfd` (W, fd), `json_dump_file` (W: `fopen` + `json_dumpf` + `fclose`).\n\n**Pack / unpack / sprintf** — `json_vpack_ex` (L), `json_pack_ex` (W), `json_pack` (W, `flags=0`, `error=NULL`); `json_vunpack_ex` (L), `json_unpack_ex` (W), `json_unpack` (W); `json_vsprintf` (L), `json_sprintf` (W).\n\n**Comparison / copying** — `json_equal`, `json_copy` (shallow), `json_deep_copy` (W over `do_deep_copy`).\n\n**Allocator hooks** — `json_set_alloc_funcs` (2-arg; sets `do_realloc = NULL`, forcing malloc+memcpy+free emulation in `jsonp_realloc`), `json_set_alloc_funcs2` (3-arg, real realloc), `json_get_alloc_funcs`, `json_get_alloc_funcs2`.\n\n**Version** — `jansson_version_str`, `jansson_version_cmp`; macros `JANSSON_MAJOR/MINOR/MICRO_VERSION`, `JANSSON_VERSION` (\"2.15.0\"), `JANSSON_VERSION_HEX`.\n\n**Error reporting** — struct `json_error_t` (`line`, `column`, `position`, `source[80]`, `text[160]`), `enum json_error_code` (18 values), inline `json_error_code()` which reads `text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH-1]`.\n\n**Internal-but-exported (no public header declaration; still ABI-visible)** —\n- `jsonp_malloc`, `jsonp_realloc`, `jsonp_free`, `jsonp_strndup`\n- `jsonp_error_init`, `jsonp_error_set`, `jsonp_error_vset`, `jsonp_error_set_source`\n- `jsonp_strtod`, `jsonp_dtostr` (locale-independent double conversion)\n- `jsonp_stringn_nocheck_own` (takes ownership of a heap buffer)\n- `jsonp_loop_check` (cycle detection; `LOOP_KEY_LEN` = `2 + sizeof(ptr)*2 + 1`)\n- `hashtable_init`, `hashtable_close`, `hashtable_set`, `hashtable_get`, `hashtable_del`, `hashtable_clear`, `hashtable_iter`, `hashtable_iter_at`, `hashtable_iter_next`, `hashtable_iter_key`, `hashtable_iter_key_len`, `hashtable_iter_value`, `hashtable_iter_set`, plus the `hashtable_key_to_iter` macro and the global `volatile uint32_t hashtable_seed`\n- `strbuffer_init`, `strbuffer_close`, `strbuffer_clear`, `strbuffer_value`, `strbuffer_steal_value`, `strbuffer_append_byte`, `strbuffer_append_bytes`, `strbuffer_pop`\n- `utf8_encode`, `utf8_check_first`, `utf8_check_full`, `utf8_iterate`, `utf8_check_string`\n- `dtoa`, `dtoa_r`, `freedtoa`, `gethex`, `dtoa_divmax`, `strtod__unused` (from `dtoa.c`)\n- `do_deep_copy`, `do_object_update_recursive` (recursive helpers taking the `parents` hashtable)\n\n---\n\n# 6. I/O-BASED ENTRY POINTS AND THE SHAPES THAT MATTER\n\n**Decode side.** All five decoders funnel into `parse_json(&lex, flags, error)` after `lex_init` installs a 1-byte-at-a-time `get_func`; the differences are the byte source, the `error->source` string, and the argument validation.\n- `json_loads` (`load.c:902`): `string_get` returns EOF at the first `'\\0'` — a NUL byte truncates the input rather than erroring. NULL input → `json_error_invalid_argument` \"wrong arguments\".\n- `json_loadb` (`load.c:943`): `buffer_get` bounds on `buflen`, so raw NULs inside the buffer are delivered to the lexer (→ control-char / invalid-token errors). NULL buffer → `json_error_invalid_argument`. `buflen == 0` → premature end of input.\n- `json_loadf` (`load.c:968`): `get` is `fgetc`; `source` is `\"<stdin>\"` when `input == stdin`, else `\"<stream>\"`; NULL → invalid argument. `fgetc` returning EOF is indistinguishable from a read error here (no `ferror` check), so a mid-document I/O failure looks like premature EOF.\n- `json_loadfd` (`load.c:1003`): `fd_get_func` does `read(*fd, &c, 1)` and treats **anything other than a return of exactly 1 as EOF** (`load.c:994-1001`) — short reads, `EINTR`, and errors all silently terminate the document. `input < 0` → invalid argument. One syscall per byte.\n- `json_load_file` (`load.c:1031`): `fopen(path, \"rb\")`; failure → `json_error_cannot_open_file` \"unable to open %s: %s\" with `strerror(errno)`; NULL path → invalid argument.\n- `json_load_callback` (`load.c:1081`): `MAX_BUF_LEN` = 1024; `callback_get` refills only when `pos >= len` and calls `stream->callback(stream->data, MAX_BUF_LEN, stream->arg)` (`load.c:1065-1079`). Return **0 or `(size_t)-1`** → EOF (both mean \"stop\"; there is no distinct error signal, so a failing callback yields a premature-EOF-style parse error). A return value **greater than 1024 is not validated** and would over-read the fixed `char data[MAX_BUF_LEN]`. Partial fills are fine (any 1..1024). NULL callback → invalid argument.\n- Cross-cutting: `JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK` is what makes streaming several documents from one FILE*/fd/callback practical, and `error->position` after a successful parse (`load.c:877-880`) is the byte offset consumed, in units counted by `stream->position` (bytes, including the buffered multi-byte tail advanced by `lex_save_cached`).\n\n**Encode side.** All six funnel into `json_dump_callback` (`dump.c:480`), which applies the `JSON_ENCODE_ANY` gate, builds the `parents_set` hashtable for cycle detection, and returns `do_dump`'s result.\n- Any callback returning **nonzero aborts immediately** and `json_dump_callback` returns -1 — but the bytes already handed to the callback have been emitted, so partial/truncated output is the observable state. Because the abort path returns early, the `parents_set` entries for the in-progress containers are not `hashtable_del`'d (they are freed wholesale by `hashtable_close`).\n- `json_dumps` (`dump.c:425`): `dump_to_strbuffer` → `strbuffer_append_bytes`, which returns -1 on OOM/overflow; on success the value is stolen and `jsonp_realloc`'d down to `length + 1` (realloc failure is tolerated — the oversized buffer is returned instead). Result is NUL-terminated; caller frees with the configured free function.\n- `json_dumpb` (`dump.c:448`, `dump_to_buffer` at `dump.c:42-50`): returns **0 on failure**, otherwise `buf.used` = the total number of bytes the encoding *would* need. Chunk-granular truncation: `if (buf->used + size <= buf->size) memcpy(...)` — a chunk that doesn't fit is skipped while `used` still advances, so with a too-small buffer you get a partially filled buffer (earlier chunks written, later short chunks possibly written after a long chunk was skipped — output is **not** a clean prefix) and a return value `> size` signalling truncation. `dump_to_buffer` never returns nonzero, so a small buffer is not an error. No NUL terminator is written. Passing `size == 0` (with any non-NULL/NULL buffer) is the standard \"measure the length\" shape. Note `struct buffer.size` is `const size_t` (`dump.c:33`).\n- `json_dumpf` (`dump.c:457`, `dump_to_file`): `fwrite(buffer, size, 1, dest) != 1` → -1. Since the item count is 1, a **short write of any amount is a total failure**, and a hypothetical zero-length chunk would also be reported as failure (no such chunk is ever produced: `dump_string` guards with `if (pos != str)`, `dump_indent` guards with `n_spaces > 0`, all literals are fixed non-zero lengths).\n- `json_dumpfd` (`dump.c:461`, `dump_to_fd`): `write(*dest, buffer, size) == (ssize_t)size` else -1 — **partial writes and `EINTR` are hard failures, with no retry loop**, and without `HAVE_UNISTD_H` the function unconditionally returns -1. Because output is emitted in many small chunks (one per token, separator, indent run, escape sequence), this is a high-syscall-count path.\n- `json_dump_file` (`dump.c:465`): `fopen(path, \"w\")` (text mode, truncating); NULL FILE → -1 with no error struct (none of the dump APIs take a `json_error_t`); `fclose` failure is reported as -1 even when the dump itself succeeded, and the return value of a successful `fclose` lets the dump's own `result` through.","usage":{"total_tokens":110614,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":363424},"uuid":"06412496-e0f2-49c8-b0d9-e9b3a7bf2e0e","session_id":"db0c18e4-4025-4f1b-bc6c-7708c62dcd24"}
