{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nk56n3j2d5em72dfnuhcnrmboy6xwgyowj566skkkdc3ej2f4rtq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_011RPG1aFGhNDoXtVGdYjzX9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"R=/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport re\nbase=\"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RK3msz/translation/\"\n\n# ---------- ERRORS.md ----------\np=base+\"ERRORS.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\n\n# H6 is genuinely unreachable: hashtable_set() computes hash_str(key, key_len)\n# BEFORE calling init_pair, so a key_len near SIZE_MAX reads SIZE_MAX bytes.\ns=s.replace(\n \"| H6 | `init_pair` | `key_len >= (size_t)-1 - offsetof(pair_t, key)` — key length overflow guard | `NULL` | [ ] |\",\n \"| H6 | `init_pair` | `key_len >= (size_t)-1 - offsetof(pair_t, key)` — key length overflow guard | `NULL` | n/a — unreachable without UB: `hashtable_set` calls `hash_str(key, key_len)` *before* `init_pair`, so reaching this guard requires first reading ~`SIZE_MAX` bytes |\")\n\n# D46 / O27 / N26 already n/a; mark the remaining unreachable asserts uniformly.\nfor row in [\"D27\",\"D28\"]:\n    s=re.sub(r\"(\\| \"+row+r\" \\|[^\\n]*?)\\| n/a \\(unreachable\\) \\|\", r\"\\1| n/a — `assert` the C treats as unreachable |\", s)\n\n# Everything else now has a passing differential test.\ns=s.replace(\"| [ ] |\",\"| [x] |\")\n\nheader_extra = \"\"\"\n## Where each row is tested\n\n| ERRORS.md section | test file / functions |\n|---|---|\n| A, B, C (value.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::a_*`, `b_*`, `c_*`; `tests/b_value.rs` |\n| D (dump.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::d*`; `tests/b_encode.rs::a35_*` |\n| E (error.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::e1_e7_*`; `tests/b_internals.rs::m6_m7_m8_*` |\n| F (memory.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::f_memory_rejections`; `tests/c_errors_oom.rs` |\n| G (strbuffer.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::g_strbuffer_guards`; `tests/b_internals.rs::j*` |\n| H (hashtable.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::h_hashtable_*`; `tests/b_internals.rs::i*` |\n| I (version.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::i1_i4_version_cmp` |\n| J (utf.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::j*`; `tests/b_internals.rs::k*` |\n| K (strconv.c / dtoa.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::k*`; `tests/b_internals.rs::l*`; `tests/d_symbols.rs` |\n| L, M (load.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::l_m_load_rejections_exact_codes`, `m_*` |\n| N, O (pack_unpack.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::n_*`, `o_unpack_rejections` |\n| P (hashtable_seed.c) | `tests/c_errors.rs::p5_p6_q11_*`; `tests/b_internals.rs::i7_*` |\n| Q (FFI boundaries) | `tests/c_errors.rs::q2_q3_*`, `q12_*` |\n| all OOM rows | `tests/c_errors_oom.rs` (budgeted allocator, budget swept 0..N) |\n\nEvery OOM row is reached by installing a *budgeted* allocator through\n`json_set_alloc_funcs` / `json_set_alloc_funcs2` on each `.so` independently and\nsweeping the budget from 0 upwards, so each allocation site inside an operation\nfails in turn. `tests/c_errors_oom.rs::zz_injection_harness_is_effective` proves\nthe injection is not vacuous, and `::oom_allocation_counts_match` additionally\nproves C and Rust issue the *same number* of allocations — which is what makes\nbudget-indexed comparison meaningful.\n\nBoth the 2-arg `json_set_alloc_funcs` (which NULLs `do_realloc` and forces the\nmalloc+memcpy+free realloc *emulation* path) and the 3-arg\n`json_set_alloc_funcs2` form are swept, since they are different code paths.\n\nRows are compared on the FULL `json_error_t` — `line`, `column`, `position`,\n`source`, the message text, and the `json_error_code` byte at `text[159]` — not\nmerely on \"both failed\".\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(\"---\\n\\n## A. `value.c` — objects\", header_extra + \"\\n---\\n\\n## A. `value.c` — objects\")\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\n\n# ---------- CONFIGS.md ----------\np=base+\"CONFIGS.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"| [ ] |\",\"| [x] |\")\nfooter = \"\"\"\n\n---\n\n## Where each row is tested\n\n| CONFIGS.md section | test file |\n|---|---|\n| A — encoding | `tests/b_encode.rs` (15 tests) |\n| B — decoding | `tests/b_decode.rs` (11 tests) |\n| C — round-trip | `tests/b_decode.rs::c1_c2_*`, `c3_*` |\n| D — objects | `tests/b_value.rs::d*` |\n| E — arrays | `tests/b_value.rs::e*` |\n| F — scalars / equality / copying | `tests/b_value.rs::f*` |\n| G — pack | `tests/b_packunpack.rs::g*` |\n| H — unpack | `tests/b_packunpack.rs::h*` |\n| I — hashtable | `tests/b_internals.rs::i*` |\n| J — strbuffer | `tests/b_internals.rs::j*` |\n| K — utf8 | `tests/b_internals.rs::k*`; `tests/c_errors.rs::j*` |\n| L — number conversion | `tests/b_internals.rs::l*`; `tests/d_symbols.rs::d_dtoa_r_direct`, `d_strtod__unused_direct`, `d_gethex_direct` |\n| M — memory / error / loop check | `tests/b_internals.rs::m*`; `tests/c_errors_oom.rs::oom_allocation_counts_match` |\n| N — version | `tests/b_internals.rs::n1_n2_version` |\n\n## Randomization\n\nEvery row is driven with many inputs, not one: `common::Rng` is a SplitMix64 PRNG\nseeded with a per-test constant, so runs are byte-for-byte reproducible. Several\naxes are covered **exhaustively** rather than randomly:\n\n- `utf8_check_first` over all 256 byte values;\n- `utf8_encode` over every codepoint `0..=0x10FFFF`;\n- `utf8_check_full` over all 2-byte sequences;\n- `JSON_INDENT(n)` for every `n` in `0..=32`;\n- `JSON_REAL_PRECISION(p)` for every `p` in `0..=32`;\n- the full 32-element decode-flag lattice;\n- `json_dumpb` at every buffer size from 0 up to the required length;\n- `json_dump_callback` aborting at every chunk index in turn.\n\n## Notes discovered while verifying\n\n- `JSON_PRESERVE_ORDER` (0x100) is referenced by **no** `.c` file — a true\n  no-op. `a16_preserve_order_is_a_noop` asserts setting it never changes output.\n- `JSON_REAL_PRECISION(p)` for `p` in `25..=31` makes **every** real fail to\n  encode: `jsonp_dtostr` hands `dtoa_r` a 25-byte scratch buffer and mode 2 with\n  `ndigits >= 25` bails out. Both libraries agree.\n- `JSON_EMBED` applies to the ROOT only (the bit is stripped before recursion),\n  and an *empty* embedded container emits nothing at all.\n- `json_dumpb` with a too-small buffer does not produce a clean prefix: a chunk\n  that does not fit is skipped while `used` still advances, so a later shorter\n  chunk can still be written. The byte-for-byte buffer comparison covers this.\n- `utf8_encode` accepts UTF-16 surrogates (`0xD800..0xDFFF`) — only\n  `utf8_check_full` rejects them.\n- Decoder underflow (`1e-400`) is silently accepted as `0.0`; only overflow is\n  an error.\n- `fopen(\"/\", \"rb\")` SUCCEEDS on Linux, so `json_load_file(\"/\")` reports a parse\n  error rather than `json_error_cannot_open_file`.\n- A *direct* self-reference cannot be built through the public API (the C rejects\n  `json == value`), so the cycle tests build indirect 2-, 3- and nested cycles.\n- `hashtable_seed` is an exported **global** (`volatile uint32_t`), not a\n  function; `json_object_seed` is one-shot and ignores later calls.\n\"\"\"\ns = s.rstrip() + footer\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nprint(\"done\")\nPY\ngrep -c '\\[x\\]' $R/translation/CONFIGS.md; grep -c '\\[x\\]' $R/translation/ERRORS.md; grep -c 'n/a' $R/translation/ERRORS.md; grep -c '\\[ \\]' $R/translation/CONFIGS.md $R/translation/ERRORS.md","description":"Mark artifact rows verified and add test mapping"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1820,"cache_read_input_tokens":418697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db0c18e4-4025-4f1b-bc6c-7708c62dcd24","uuid":"a7f5f8d3-f490-4942-bc54-68c3351c0408","timestamp":"2026-09-08T15:57:07.077Z"}
