n":"not_first_party","subagent_stats":{"spawned":3,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":3},"started_in_background":3,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":3,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"Explore":3}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":116,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Verification complete. **146 tests, 0 failures, 0 behavioural divergences** between the C and Rust jansson.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — 0 missing symbols | **130 / 130**, diff empty both ways; enforced as a test plus `dlsym` resolvability |\n| Phase B — every `CONFIGS.md` row | **192 rows, all ✓** |\n| C vs Rust binary stdout | **n/a** — `CMakeLists.txt` has no `add_executable`, `Cargo.toml` no `[[bin]]`. Library I/O still byte-compared (file bytes for `dumpf`/`dumpfd`/`dump_file`; *chunk-boundary* sequences for `dump_callback`) |\n| Phase C — every `ERRORS.md` row | **all ✓**; 22 rows `n/a` with stated reason (C `assert`s / genuine UB). Compared on the full `json_error_t` incl. the `json_error_code` byte at `text[159]` |\n| Every feature combination | `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` and `src/` has no `cfg(feature=…)` → powerset is the empty set; `(default)`, `--no-default-features`, `--all-features` all **PASS** |\n\n## Why the green result is trustworthy\n\nA passing suite proves nothing unless a wrong translation fails it. `mutation_sweep.sh` injects one off-by-one/wrong-constant bug at a time and requires failure: **14 injected, 14 caught, 0 escaped** across `dump/utf/value/load/hashtable/strbuffer/strconv`.\n\nOne mutation (`json_array_get` bound `>=`→`>`) initially **escaped** — `table[entries]` in a fresh `malloc` is usually already zero, so the out-of-range read returns `NULL` and looks correct. I added tests that plant a *live, non-NULL* pointer in that slot first (append → keep a ref → remove), which makes the off-by-one observable; same hardening for object lookups after delete. Also, `c_errors_oom.rs::zz_injection_harness_is_effective` proves the budgeted allocator really forces failures, so the OOM rows can't pass vacuously — and `oom_allocation_counts_match` shows both libraries issue the *same number* of allocations.\n\n## Notable\n\n- **No `translation/src/` changes were needed.** Every fix this session was to a *test* that had encoded a wrong assumption about the C — e.g. a direct self-reference is impossible (C rejects `json == value`), `[99999999999999999999]` is *accepted* under `JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL`, `fopen(\"/\")` succeeds on Linux, and `json_error_item_not_found` fires before format errors.\n- Two of my probes were genuine C UB, not testable rejections, and are marked `n/a`: `json_dump_callback` with a NULL callback (dereferenced once the root passes the `JSON_ENCODE_ANY` gate), and `init_pair`'s `key_len` guard (unreachable because `hash_str` reads `key_len` bytes first).\n- `--test-threads=1` is mandatory: the C `dtoa()` has a non-thread-safe global freelist and `json_set_alloc_funcs*` mutates global hooks.\n- `cargo` runs `--offline` (crates.io is proxy-blocked; `libloading 0.8.9` was already in the local registry cache).\n\nArtifacts in `translation/`: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, plus `run_tests.sh`, `run_all_features.sh`, `mutation_sweep.sh` and ~11k lines of tests. `c_src/` is unmodified.","ttft_ms":3148,"type":"result","duration_ms":5498234,"uuid":"0f238e1e-0f01-4c61-961a-66269b70264e","ttft_stream_ms":3063,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":3063,"queued_turn_count":0}
